Ladybird now passes fontconfig the language most likely written in a
code point’s script when it asks for a fallback font. In the CI image
with fonts-noto-core and fonts-unifont installed, the pattern without a
language matches Unifont for Thai, Devanagari, Arabic, Georgian and
Ethiopic, and with th, hi, ar, ka and am it matches Noto Sans Thai, Noto
Sans Devanagari, Noto Kufi Arabic, Noto Sans Georgian and Noto Sans
Ethiopic. Chrome’s Linux fallback, gfx::GetFallbackFontForChar(), sorts
the fonts for the element’s locale (FontDescription::LocaleOrDefault())
and takes the first with the character: That gives Noto Sans Thai for
en-US and th, but Unifont for ja. Gecko derives the language from the
character itself, with gfxPlatformFontList::GetFontPrefLangFor(), and
searches that language’s preferred fonts, as Ladybird now does with the
script’s likely language.
